Description: sdio: transmit without copying frames
 Every SDIO data frame was copied twice on its way to the card: the driver
 advertised no scatter-gather, so the stack segmented TCP by copying and
 checksumming the payload, and the TX aggregation copied each frame again
 into one buffer for a single CMD53. The vendor's CONFIG_SDIO_ADMA path
 hands the frames to the host as a scatter list instead, but it was dead
 code on mainline kernels: it calls sdio_write_sg() (only some vendor
 kernels have it), its list type was commented out, its header and fill
 buffers were module memory (not DMA-able), and its frame layout ("fake"
 header bytes) is rejected by the AIC8801 firmware.
 .
 - aicwf_sdio_write_sg(): one CMD53 block write of a scatterlist through
   mmc_wait_for_req(), with the R5 checks and limits of the core's helper;
   named so that it does not clash with vendor kernels' sdio_write_sg().
 - aicwf_adma_add(): the frame laid out as the copying path does (4-byte
   SDIO header, CRC8 on the D80 family, then descriptor and data, padded
   to 4 bytes); the header goes into the skb headroom, the linear part and
   each page fragment are list entries; a frame that would not fit the
   host's segments is linearized, and dropped if that fails.
 - The fill buffer is allocated; the list is reset before the tx state is
   freed (it was reset after: a use-after-free).
 - NETIF_F_SG | NETIF_F_HW_CSUM: the driver computes the checksum
   (skb_checksum_help), the chip has none; the copying path uses
   skb_copy_bits() for fragmented frames.
 .
 CONFIG_SDIO_ADMA stays n by default; set it to y to use the scatter list.
 Measured on a NanoKVM (SG2002, AIC8801 at 50 MHz SDIO, Linux 7.3) with this
 tree: TCP transmit over Wi-Fi, CPU busy per Mbit/s, base 1.10 (4 runs),
 with this patch and CONFIG_SDIO_ADMA=y 0.97 (6 runs, -12%) at 63-74
 Mbit/s; no frame copy left in the profile. Also tested on an AIC8800D80
 board. SHA-256 of 100 MB sent intact; association after driver reloads,
 AP mode.
